 "long-wave.md": "---\ntitle: Long wave, 171 kHz\ndate: 2026-10-28\nsummary: A night drive, a dashboard, and a display that glowed like this one.\ntags: [design]\n---\nSample post. Replace it with your own words.\n\nThe first frame of this site is a photo: a car radio at night, tuned to long wave, its display glowing through the mesh.\n\n![The display that started this](assets/lw-171.png)\n\n## Why long wave\n\nLong wave travels far and carries little. A few words, a long way. It seemed like the right band for writing that is not in a hurry.\n\nPictures in a post come through the same dots as everything else. They lose their colour and most of their detail, and keep their shape.\n",
 "notes-from-the-dial.md": "---\ntitle: Notes from the dial\ndate: 2026-08-14\nsummary: Short things that did not need a whole post.\ntags: [notes]\n---\nSample post. Replace it with your own words.\n\n- A note.\n- Another note, slightly longer than the first one.\n- A link to something worth reading: [example.com](https://example.com).\n",
 "small-interfaces.md": "---\ntitle: Small interfaces\ndate: 2026-09-30\nsummary: The best boundary is the one you can explain in a sentence.\ntags: [code]\n---\nSample post. Replace it with your own words.\n\nA module that does one thing behind a small surface is easy to replace, easy to test, and easy to forget about, which is the point.\n\nUse `one interface` per concept, and let the details stay where they belong.\n",
 "writing-it-down.md": "---\ntitle: Writing it down once\ndate: 2026-10-12\nsummary: Conventions that live in files outlast the conversations that made them.\ntags: [conventions, agents]\n---\nSample post. Replace it with your own words.\n\nEvery repository grows its own habits. Some of them are good, and most of them live only in someone's head.\n\n## What goes in the file\n\n- how a change is proposed and reviewed\n- what a finished pull request looks like\n- which checks must pass, and why\n\n> A rule nobody can find is a rule nobody follows.\n\nOnce it is written down, a person or an agent can apply it the same way every time. The tool that does this for my repositories is [Repo Standards](https://repo-standards.com/):\n\n```\nnpx @lutzseverino/repo-standards adopt <source>\n```\n\n## What stays out\n\nTaste. A file can say *what* a good change contains. It cannot make anyone care about the reader, and it should not pretend to.\n\nThe rest of this post is placeholder text, long enough to show how a longer piece reads on this screen: how it scrolls, pages or prints, depending on the variant. Short paragraphs read best here. Long ones turn into a wall of dots, which is a good reason to keep them short anyway.\n\nA second paragraph, to fill the page. And a third sentence in it, so the wrap has something to do.\n"
